    "snippet": "---\ntitle: 2020 United States federal government data breach - Wikipedia\ndate: 2020-12-14\n---\nduration: At least 8SolarWinds Orion: More US government agencies hacked December 15, 2020 BBC December 16, 2020 December 16, 2020 live or 9 months\ndate: Before October 2019 (start of supply chain compromise), March 2020 (possible federal breach start date), December 13, 2020 (breach acknowledged)\nlocation: United States, United Kingdom, Spain, Israel, United Arab Emirates, Canada, Mexico, othersMicrosoft warns UK companies were targeted by SolarWinds hackers James Cook The Telegraph December 18, 2020 subscription December 21, 2020 April 19, 2021 live\ntarget: U.S. federal government, state and local governments, and private sector\ntype: Cyberattack, data breach\ntheme: Malware, backdoor, advanced persistent threat, espionage\ncause: SolarWinds supply chain attack (SUNBURST trojan), Microsoft Outlook Web App software bug, Microsoft supply chain attack (reseller compromise), VMware software bug, Zerologon software bug\nfirst_reporter: FireEye (coordinated vulnerability disclosure), NSA (coordinated vulnerability disclosure), Reuters (public disclosure)\nsuspects: Berserk Bear (Russia), Cozy Bear (Russia), FSB (Russia), SVR (Russia)\n\nIn 2020, a major cyberattack suspected to have been committed by a group backed by the Russian government penetrated thousands of organizations globally including multiple parts of the United States federal government, leading to a series of data breaches. The cyberattack and data breach were reported to be among the worst cyber-espionage incidents ever suffered by the U.S., due to the sensitivity and high profile of the targets and the long duration (eight to nine months) in which the hackers had access. Within days of its discovery, at least 200 organizations around the world had been reported to be affected by the attack, and some of these may also have suffered data breaches. Affected organizations worldwide included NATO, the U.K. government, the European Parliament, Microsoft and others.\n\nThe attack, which had gone undetected for months, was first publicly reported on December 13, 2020, and was initially only known to have affected the U.S. Treasury Department and the National Telecommunications and Information Administration (NTIA), part of the U.S. Department of Commerce."
